---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import logo from "../../assets/negahban.png";
import PageHeader from "../../components/PageHeader.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

const updated = "2026-10-01";
---
<Layout
	title="negahban – Privacy Policy"
	description="Privacy policy, terms of use, and data deletion instructions for negahban, a personal Instagram comment moderation tool."
>
	<Image
		src={logo}
		alt="negahban: a panda on sentry duty"
		width={160}
		height={160}
		class="logo-rounded"
	/>
	<PageHeader
		eyebrow="negahban"
		title="Privacy policy"
		lede={`Privacy policy, terms of use, and data deletion instructions. Last updated ${updated}.`}
	/>
	<div class="prose">

		<p>
			<b>negahban</b> (Persian for <i>watchman</i>) is a personal,
			self-hosted command-line tool that helps the owner of an Instagram
			professional account moderate the comments on their own posts. It
			is operated by Elaheh Dastan for her own account only. It is not a
			hosted service, has no users other than its operator, and is not
			offered to third parties.
		</p>

		<h3>What data it accesses</h3>
		<p>
			Through Meta's official Instagram API, with the account owner's
			explicit authorization, negahban reads:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>the owner's own posts (id, caption, permalink, timestamp, comment count);</li>
			<li>
				comments and replies on those posts: comment id, text, timestamp,
				the commenter's username, and whether the comment is hidden.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			It does not access direct messages, follower lists, profile
			information of commenters beyond their username, or any data from
			accounts other than the owner's.
		</p>

		<h3>How the data is used</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Comment text (together with the post caption, for context) is
				sent to Anthropic's Claude API to be classified as acceptable,
				civil criticism, abuse, spam, or a scam. Anthropic processes this
				data under its
				<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/commercial-terms" target="_blank" rel="noopener">commercial terms</a>
				and does not train on it.
			</li>
			<li>
				Based on that classification and the owner's settings, negahban
				may hide, unhide, or delete a comment on the owner's own post, or
				simply flag it for the owner to look at. Civil criticism is never
				hidden.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h3>Where the data is stored</h3>
		<p>
			Everything stays on the operator's own computer. negahban keeps a
			local audit log (an SQLite file) with each judged comment's id,
			text, username, the classification, and the action taken, so that
			decisions can be reviewed and reversed. Access tokens are kept in
			the operator's local password manager. No data is uploaded to any
			server other than Meta's API (to read and act on comments) and
			Anthropic's API (to classify comment text). Nothing is shared with
			or sold to anyone.
		</p>

		<h3>Retention</h3>
		<p>
			The local audit log is kept as long as the operator finds it
			useful and can be deleted at any time. Classification requests to
			Anthropic are not retained by negahban.
		</p>

		<h3 id="deletion">Data deletion</h3>
		<p>
			If you commented on one of the operator's posts and want the record
			of your comment removed from negahban's local log, email
			<b>elahe.dstn at gmail dot com</b> with your Instagram username; the
			corresponding rows will be deleted within 30 days. Deleting your
			comment on Instagram itself is done through Instagram, not through
			negahban. The operator can revoke negahban's access to the account
			at any time in Instagram under
			<i>Settings → Website permissions → Apps and websites</i>, which
			invalidates its tokens.
		</p>

		<h3 id="terms">Terms of use</h3>
		<p>
			negahban is open source and provided as is, without warranty. It
			is intended to be run by the owner of an Instagram professional
			account on that account only, in compliance with Meta's Platform
			Terms and Developer Policies.
		</p>

		<h3>Contact</h3>
		<p>
			Questions about this policy: <b>elahe.dstn at gmail dot com</b>.
		</p>
	</div>
</Layout>
